  "posts": [
    {
      "text": "[1/4] I’ve aligned the first three articles in my accessibility series: define the evaluation scope, examine non-text content, then compare prerecorded audio and silent video with their alternatives. The same reading structure now connects all four language editions.",
      "url": "https://x.com/effloow/status/2107231335060488590"
    },
    {
      "text": "[2/4] Start with one user process and evidence you can actually inspect. The overview prompt helps plan criterion-level review. A plan is not a conformance result; unread materials and unverified states still need to be recorded.",
      "url": "https://x.com/effloow/status/2107231336641745061"
    },
    {
      "text": "[3/4] For images, identify the purpose at each use: information, decoration, action or a complex explanation. The non-text-content article puts the observed prompt results before the checklist, then explains how to supply evidence for your own review.",
      "url": "https://x.com/effloow/status/2107231338399162385"
    },
    {
      "text": "[4/4] The audio and silent-video article follows the same path: explanation, visible practice pages and recorded findings, then the checklist and prompt guidance. Closings connect the full learning sequence and adjacent topics. English overview link below.",
      "url": "https://x.com/effloow/status/2107231339909140905"
    }
  ],
